Porting console decoration to other framebuffer drivers
======================================================

This describes the Newport implementation merged into xiri in November 2026
and a proposed approach for extending it.  Only Newport currently implements
the decoration.  NEWPORT_DECOR is a driver option, not a generic wscons API.
The feature is a live text console with a fixed background; text, erasure,
scrolling, cursor movement, and virtual-screen restoration all participate.

The default scheme is black at the top, Octane-style green (#008060) at the
bottom, and white kernel messages.  GENERIC32_IP2x enables it for Newport;
GENERIC64_IP2x and GENERIC64_32_IP2x inherit that setting.  Other framebuffer
drivers in those kernels receive white kernel text but no gradient.

All source paths below are relative to the repository root.  The companion
tests/dev/wscons/README.newport-decor documents the manual regression suite
and the emulator revisions actually tested.

Reference implementation
------------------------

sys/arch/sgimips/gio/newport.c:

    newport_setup_decor_cmap  Reserve gradient and inverse-cursor colours.
    newport_fill_background  Paint a rectangle at absolute screen positions.
    newport_fill_rectangle   Use colour-index drawing for decorated fills.
    newport_putchar          Restore the cell background, then draw the glyph.
    newport_erasecols        Restore backgrounds for partial-line erasure.
    newport_eraserows        Restore rows and, on full clears, screen margins.
    newport_init_screen     Enable decoration and vcons redraw-on-copy flags.
    newport_attach          Paint the initial background and replay boot text.
    newport_cursor          Invert pixels in place, preserving the background.
    newport_setup_hw        Restore the appropriate palette for the mode.
    newport_ioctl           Restore hardware and text on return to emulation.

sys/arch/sgimips/gio/files.gio declares NEWPORT_DECOR in opt_newport.h.
share/man/man4/man4.sgimips/newport.4 describes its user-visible behaviour.
sys/dev/wscons/wsdisplay_vcons.c and wsdisplay_vconsvar.h supply the saved
characters/attributes, redraw callbacks, and VCONS_NO_COPY* flags.

Rendering contract
------------------

Use one background painter for glyph cells, erase operations, full clears,
and margins.  Its output must depend on absolute visible-screen coordinates,
not the rectangle being repainted, the text row, or the scroll offset.  Two
partial paints must produce the same pixels as one paint of their union.

Newport uses N = 112 horizontal bands across the visible height H.  For a
pixel row y, with 0 <= y < H, the band and its colour are:

    band = floor(y * N / H)
    colour(band) = (0, floor(128 * band / (N - 1)),
                      floor(96 * band / (N - 1)))

A band ends at ceil((band + 1) * H / N).  Splitting rectangles at those
boundaries keeps partial erasure identical to full-screen painting.  A new
driver should clip rectangles to its visible bounds, reject empty areas,
use arithmetic wide enough for its dimensions, and handle small modes when
choosing N.  Include rasops ri_xorigin/ri_yorigin when mapping text cells.
Convert to the hardware coordinate system only at the drawing boundary.

For each glyph, decode the effective background attribute after reverse
video handling.  If it is black, restore the gradient under the entire cell
and draw only foreground glyph pixels.  Otherwise render a solid background
and the glyph normally.  Spaces must erase old pixels too; underlined spaces
must still draw the underline.  Keep highlight, reverse video, font strides,
and supported character widths working.

Black is the transparency key, including explicitly requested ANSI black.
The current implementation does not distinguish that from the terminal's
default background.  Do not substitute zero-valued pixel tests for decoding
attributes unless the target driver's colour mapping guarantees equivalence.
Newport's attribute shifts are rasops-specific; older drivers can encode
foreground/background colours differently.

If glyphs are cached as fully rendered rectangles, their background becomes
position-dependent.  Bypass that cache for decorated cells or cache glyph
masks and composite at the destination.  Copying a cached cell from another
screen position can copy the wrong gradient.

Saved text and scrolling
------------------------

Moving framebuffer pixels also moves the background baked into those pixels.
For a first port, redraw characters from saved text at their destination.
In a vcons driver's init_screen callback, install the decorated drawing
operations and set these flags on every decorated screen:

    scr->scr_flags |= VCONS_NO_COPYCOLS | VCONS_NO_COPYROWS;

vcons then uses putchar-based copies while maintaining its text buffer.
Keep the accelerated copy operations available for the option-disabled path.
Although a purely vertical gradient can permit horizontal copies, disabling
both paths initially avoids dependencies on the background's shape.

A driver with direct wsdisplay textops but no saved text cannot obtain this
behaviour by setting flags alone.  First migrate it to vcons, preserving its
existing plain console behaviour, or provide equivalent character/attribute
storage and redraw support.  Prefer vcons to a second buffer implementation.

Do not replace the two flags with VCONS_DONT_READ without reviewing cursors.
VCONS_DONT_READ also selects VCONS_NO_CURSOR and its putchar-based cursor.
The current vcons_cursor_noread handling can leave cursor pixels behind when
hiding it with all DONT_READ flags set.  Newport retains its own reversible
in-place inversion.  A separate hardware cursor plane is another option.
Every strategy must restore the exact prior cell when hiding or moving.

Pixel formats and palettes
--------------------------

For indexed colour, identify the entries actually used by terminal text and
reserve them before allocating the ramp.  They need not be palette indices
0 through 15: Newport uses RGB332 indices derived from rasops_cmap.  It also
reserves each text entry's index XOR 0xff for the pixel-inverting cursor.
The remaining pairs supply 112 gradient entries and their RGB complements.

This count and XOR pairing are Newport-specific.  Recompute the budget from
the target's colour mapping, depth, cursor operation, and other palette
users.  Use fewer bands or a different cursor strategy if necessary.  Keep
software palette copies and GETCMAP/PUTCMAP behaviour consistent where the
driver exposes them.  Audit extended-colour and anti-aliased text paths as
well as the basic terminal colours before calling any entries unused.

Verify that fills and glyphs select compatible pixel interpretation and
write masks.  Newport's decorated fills use colour-index drawing without
RGB fast clear; simply changing the palette while keeping its old RGB fill
path did not provide the required rendering behaviour.

For direct-colour framebuffers, pack the ramp using the driver's actual
channel sizes/positions, byte order, stride, and write masks.  Palette
reservation may be unnecessary, but gamma tables or indexed overlay planes
can still be shared with graphics applications.  Pixel inversion must avoid
damaging reserved or alpha bits.  Alpha glyphs must blend against the actual
gradient at each destination pixel instead of a single constant background.
For a simple initial port, selecting a supported bitmap font is an alternative
to implementing the alpha path immediately; document that limitation.

Attachment and mode transitions
-------------------------------

Keep early console output usable before saved text and full driver state
exist.  Newport leaves early cnattach on its plain drawing path, enables
decoration during vcons screen initialization, paints the full display, and
replays the kernel message buffer after attachment.  New drawing callbacks
must respect the target's console locking and FIFO rules and avoid sleeping
allocations during output, including polling/debugger paths.

Make initialization work for both the console device and screens allocated
later.  Keep palette/mode state per device and redraw flags per screen.  On
virtual-screen switches, repaint the selected screen's saved content at the
same absolute background coordinates.  RI_FULLCLEAR plus a suitable full
eraserows callback also restores margins outside the text area.

Treat WSDISPLAYIO_SMODE as a transition of ownership.  Graphics mode needs
the hardware/palette state expected by graphics clients.  Returning to
WSDISPLAYIO_MODE_EMUL must restore console depth, drawing state, and the
decoration palette before vcons_redraw_screen draws the active screen.
Audit blank/unblank, resume, font changes, and geometry changes too: any path
that resets hardware or clears pixels needs to reconstruct the console.

For a new driver, add its own option declaration and generated option header,
guard the new state/drawing paths, and start with an opt-in configuration.
Test enabled and disabled kernels before changing that driver's defaults.
White WS_KERNEL_FG is a kernel configuration choice that can affect other
console drivers in the same kernel; it is separate from enabling decoration.

Likely sgimips ports
-------------------

XZ/GR2: sys/arch/sgimips/gio/grtwo.c

    This driver uses direct wsdisplay textops, rejects extra screens, and
    lacks an SMODE restoration path.  Start with a vcons conversion and
    verify plain text, copying, and attachment before adding decoration.
    Its GR2_ATTR_* encoding differs from rasops attributes.  Its glyph
    commands consume rows bottom-to-top and use a different Y origin;
    preserve the existing glyph/XMAP fixes when adapting the renderer.
    Audit fill limits: attachment currently clears one text row at a time
    because of a documented large-fill problem.  The VC1 hardware cursor
    can avoid Newport's inverse-palette scheme if it remains independent of
    the decorated pixels.  Verify its palette/position handling separately.

Light/LG1/LG2: sys/arch/sgimips/gio/light.c

    This also uses direct textops and rejects extra screens.  It needs saved
    text before decorated scrolling, uses LIGHT_ATTR_* encoding, and has a
    stub cursor callback.  Review the REX opaque-pattern mode when painting
    foreground glyphs over a restored background.  Its SVIDEO unblank path
    currently clears the display; a decorated console would need to restore
    saved text and background there.  Establish cursor and mode-restoration
    behaviour as part of the port.

O2/CRM: sys/arch/sgimips/dev/crmfb.c

    This already uses vcons and redraws on return to emulation mode.  The
    main integration points are crmfb_init_screen, crmfb_putchar,
    crmfb_putchar_aa, erasecols/eraserows, setup_palette, and SMODE handling.
    Use sc_console_depth and the rasops channel layout, not an assumption
    that every console is 8-bit.  Both glyph paths currently fill a solid
    cell background; the alpha path then blends over that cell.  Both need
    the background painter, and the bitmap path needs transparent stippling
    for black backgrounds.  Review the inversion cursor for each depth.

Software-rendered linear framebuffers can follow the same vcons contract,
but need a renderer that preserves background pixels where glyph bits are
clear.  Wrapping a background fill around an opaque rasops putchar alone
will paint the gradient and immediately overwrite it with a solid cell.

Validation before enabling another driver
-----------------------------------------

Reuse tests/dev/wscons/newport_decor.c: despite the name, it drives terminal
escape sequences and wsdisplay ioctls rather than Newport registers.  Follow
README.newport-decor for the command sequence and exact image comparisons.
Record unsupported operations on an incomplete port; an ioctl failure is not
a successful mode-restoration test.

Verify boot/replay, partial erasure, full clears and margins, forward/reverse
scrolling, column insertion/deletion, colours, underline, reverse video,
cursor show/hide/movement, VT switches, and mapped-to-emulation restoration.
Also scroll nonblank text to verify character order and attributes.  The
current blank-scroll comparisons specifically detect background movement
and do not establish correct nonblank text movement on a newly ported driver.
Test supported fonts, depths, resolutions, and blank/resume paths separately.

Compare visible RGB captures, not just framebuffer indices: a broken palette
can leave index images looking correct.  Wait for the exerciser's DONE marker
and rendering completion; fixed short delays can capture half a redraw.
After a full vcons redraw, hide the cursor again before comparing pixels
(the current exerciser uses m followed by c).  Check gradient endpoints and
monotonic colour progression as well as text legibility near the bottom.

Use disposable disk/NVRAM copies when testing with Iris and Prismatic, and
record the kernel/emulator revisions, graphics model, and build settings.
The existing Newport results do not qualify another framebuffer or physical
hardware.  Measure redraw cost and verify FIFO ordering on the target board;
redrawing every character is more work than a hardware scroll copy.

Once a second driver works, consider extracting shared gradient geometry
and policy into a wscons helper.  Keep pixel packing, palette ownership,
hardware rendering, and cursor strategy in the drivers until their common
requirements are demonstrated by working implementations.
